# Documentation Conventions

## Naming

Markdown documentation uses SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE: `ROADMAP.md`, `PHASE_0.md`,
`DOCUMENTATION_MAP.md`. Phase-scoped deliverables carry the number as
`PHASE_<n>_NOTES.md`; the roadmap plan they pair with is `PHASE_<n>.md`.

This applies to Markdown only. Source files, JSON configs, and dotfiles keep
their existing names — those are load-bearing. `config.py` reads `config.json`
by name, and every guide documents the lowercase `config_*.json` paths.

## Layout

- `docs/roadmap/` — high-level roadmap and per-phase plans
- `docs/deliverables/` — phase deliverables
- `*_DRAFT.md` is promoted to its final name at phase completion via `git mv`

The four guides still in the repository root and the one under
`tests/benchmark/` move into `docs/` after Phase 0. Do not treat their location
as rot; it is scheduled work.

## Links

Use relative Markdown links, not Obsidian wikilinks. The vault is configured for
this (`useMarkdownLinks`, `newLinkFormat: relative`) so docs render correctly on
GitHub as well as in Obsidian. Verify that relative paths resolve after any
rename or move.

## Claims

Documentation in this repo makes assertions the code contradicts — missing
files, unparseable JSON samples, a response shape the server does not return.
Verify a claim against the tree before repeating it, and cite the file and line
when recording a finding.
